ugh the final margin was close.", "moments": [{"turn": 11, "text": "GPT-6.1 Sol's G4 took Black from +0.9 to -7.4, an 8.3-point loss, and the engine preferred D6. It was the first big slip, and Black spent most of the game after it playing from behind."}, {"turn": 17, "text": "F3 cost 18.0 points, dropping Black from -0.8 to -18.7, with the engine's choice at H5. The 20k's reply E3 at move 18 then lost 14.9 points of its own, and the lead began to swing wildly."}, {"turn": 21, "text": "After the 20k's F4 at move 20 had swung the game to +5.0 for Black, GPT-6.1 Sol's B5 gave back 15.9 points, going from +5.0 to -10.9. The engine wanted F6."}, {"turn": 29, "text": "B7 lost 18.9 points and sent Black from -0.8 to -19.7, where the engine's choice was G1. It was the start of a run of big losses at moves 31, 33 and 37, each around 16 to 20 points."}, {"turn": 42, "text": "The 20k's C1 lost 23.3 points, the largest loss in the game, moving the score from -25.7 to -2.4 for Black. Black's B1 at move 41 had lost 21.3 points just before, so this pair of moves left Black only 2.4 points behind."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "Every script is a helper: the `inspect` function parses the board and computes chains, liberties, empty regions and the effect of one move the player named; the replay script validates the move record and builds the SGF; the rest reads files, logs and writes notes. Nothing loops over candidate moves, ranks them or plays sequences forward, and the player's own reviews and `current-position-liberties.js` also say there was no move search.", "stop": false}}